\details{
The model fitted for each subset of \code{by} is

\deqn{y = \mu + \mathrm{block} + A + B + AB + \varepsilon}

with \code{A}, \code{B} the entries of \code{factors}, \code{AB} present only when
\code{interaction = TRUE} and the block term present only when \code{block} is given.
This is the ordinary randomized-complete-block factorial analysis, which is
how drought-rewatering experiments are almost always laid out.

Splitting \strong{by variable and by cycle} rather than pooling is deliberate.
Repeated cycles measured on the same plants are not independent, so a single
model that treated cycle as another crossed factor would understate the
standard errors. Analysing each cycle separately keeps every model within one
level of the repeated factor, and the change \emph{between} cycles is then a
question for \code{\link[=memory_trend]{memory_trend()}} and \code{\link[=memory_effect]{memory_effect()}}, which are built for it.
To model the cycles jointly anyway, see \emph{Repeated measures across cycles}
below: \code{cycle} moves into \code{factors} and the plant becomes a random intercept,
which is the correct way to keep the dependence in the model rather than
ignoring it.

Four things come back for each subset:
\itemize{
\item \code{anova}: the sequential (Type I) ANOVA table. With a balanced design, which
is the usual case here, Type I, II and III sums of squares coincide; when
the design is unbalanced the \code{balanced} column of \verb{$models} flags it, and
the term order in \code{factors} then matters.
\item \code{means}: cell means with standard errors and confidence intervals built on
the \strong{residual} standard error and residual degrees of freedom of the
model, not on the within-cell standard deviation, so the intervals use the
pooled error term the ANOVA is testing against. With a random effect the
means are model based and their standard errors come from the fixed-effect
covariance matrix, so they carry the estimated variance components.
\item \code{comparisons}: all pairwise contrasts of the cell means with Tukey's
honest-significant-difference p-values, then adjusted again with
\code{p_adjust} across the subsets.
\item \code{diagnostics}: Shapiro-Wilk on the residuals and Bartlett's test of
homogeneity across cells, plus \code{model_type} and \code{p_value_method} so it is
always visible which model produced the numbers above. These are
diagnostics, not gatekeepers: with the replication typical of an
ecophysiology experiment they have little power, so read them together with
the residual plot rather than as a pass or fail.
